In article <balaji.762533936@yorku.ca> balaji@nexus.yorku.ca (Balaji) writes:
>Psmith the chaotic priest who kept me entertained over this full moon
>weekend, performed a wererat sacrifice on level 4, which summons
>Juiblex. Fine, he thinks, so later down I'm going to find the Juiblex
>level deserted. Not so! Juiblex shows up on his level and engulfs
>Psmith, who however has what it takes to defend himself against the
>Juiblex attack. Recovered the amulet, came back up to level 4, and here
>Juiblex is again! Isn't this a bug?

The fact that killing a summonned &-lord doesn't destroy the
home-level version isn't a bug--it's a feature.  Killing physical
manifestations doesn't kill the real thing (an old D&D rule, probably
dating back to even earlier mythology).  Similarly, what happenned to
you in the _first_part of your story (seeing two live Jubilexes on
different levels) can be explained by the level-4 version being just a
manifestation. 

However, what happenned to you later, Jubilex's physical
manifestations staying around after the original is killed, is (IMO) a
bug.  Perhaps killing demons on their home level could be treated like 
genociding a monster type?

This game has features to handle so many possiblilities, I actually
get quite surprised when one doesn't work "properly".
